WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.768
Serialization is the process of converting application data structures
into a format that can be stored, transmitted, logged, cached, queued, or
exchanged with another system.

2
00:00:10.768 --> 00:00:18.398
Deserialization is the reverse process: reading serialized data and
turning it back into structures the application can use.

3
00:00:18.398 --> 00:00:28.024
Python applications do this constantly in APIs, files, queues, caches,
configuration, databases, background jobs, ML pipelines, and service
integrations.

4
00:00:28.024 --> 00:00:33.658
A useful distinction is the difference between plain data formats and
object serialization.

5
00:00:33.658 --> 00:00:41.003
Plain data formats, such as JSON or CSV, generally describe values:
strings, numbers, arrays, rows, fields, and records.

6
00:00:41.003 --> 00:00:49.417
Object serialization may try to preserve richer application objects,
including class names, object graphs, or reconstruction behavior.

7
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:57.118
That convenience can be valuable in controlled internal contexts, but it
becomes risky when the data crosses trust boundaries.

8
00:00:57.118 --> 00:01:06.246
Serialized data often crosses a boundary because it moves between users,
services, files, storage systems, queues, caches, partners, and processes.

9
00:01:06.246 --> 00:01:14.090
Each boundary can change who controls the data, how fresh it is, what
assumptions apply, and what the receiving code will do with it.

10
00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:21.863
The safest design starts by asking where the data came from, who owns it,
and what authority it should have after deserialization.

11
00:01:21.863 --> 00:01:24.644
A successful parse is not the same as safe data.

12
00:01:24.644 --> 00:01:34.342
Parsed data may still contain unexpected fields, wrong types, excessive
size, stale values, sensitive content, or meanings the application should
not accept.

13
00:01:34.342 --> 00:01:47.320
Safe serialization work combines format selection, safe parser
configuration, validation, type control, authorization where relevant,
resource limits, privacy-aware logging, and reviewable error handling.
